  "implementation": [
    "Pinned original BatchContentContext passes applyContentReplace output unchanged to BookHelp.saveContent/writeText. Batch save supplies no HTTP response/redirect document, does not run single-page HtmlFormatter and does not refetch media. Original output remains byte-exact as the cached .txt body and retained .raw.html callback fragment.",
    "Existing ChapterFetcher resolveContent/sanitizer/SwiftSoup normalization produces a separate .normalized.xhtml for static images, usehtml and embedded audio/video/iframe; existing ordinary reader and cached TXT export consume the same files/data contract. No new renderer, parser, persistence file/queue/cache, JSC or HTTP owner.",
    "Explicit actual chapter raw URL plus its DTO base/source URL supplies the resource base. The same existing SwiftSoup tree resolves supported src and video poster only in the render representation. Original callback bytes are untouched and no response URL is invented.",
    "Relative image/media request headers are carried by the existing ReaderHTMLUtilities/OnlineImageRequestOptions path before URL-option suffix stripping. Optional contentBaseURL defaults nil on every existing caller, preserving old behavior.",
    "The existing five-file same-queue CAS accepts actual raw/normalized candidate strings. Equal callback body can upgrade absent render files, complete identical files write nothing, and actual raw/normalized bytes are checked before ACK. Metadata-only legacy migration does not claim or validate unknown orphan render files.",
    "Current App UUID/TOC index/raw URL/generation/grant/deadline/cancel and Native effect-seal claim still guard actual file replacement. Original getter state restores the exact callback body. Unresolved image UrlOption.js refuses before file writes instead of borrowing legacy JSC.",
    "Pure direct audio URL strings and their static request options remain exact and use the existing DirectChapterAudioResolver contract; this is no claim of complete dedicated audio/video/manga owner acceptance."
  ],
